TALK = [
("R", "[warmly] Welcome back to K.R.A.P., ninety-seven point seven F.M., the official radio of Apo-jee-uh. I'm Rocky."),
("B", "And I'm Britta. And we've got something a little different for you right now. No music for a few minutes. Just us, talking about the thing that made this whole station possible in the first place."),
("R", "Pirate radio."),
("B", "Pirate radio. And before anybody gets nervous, we're an internet stream, so we're pirates in spirit only. The eyepatch is optional."),
("R", "[chuckles] The spirit part is the important bit, though. Because pirate radio was never really about breaking rules for the fun of it. It was about a simple problem. There were people with something to say, and music worth hearing, and nobody holding the door open."),
("B", "Right. So let's go back. Nineteen sixty-four. In Britain, if you wanted to hear a record on the radio, you basically had the BBC, and the BBC only played so much recorded music. There were limits. Rock and roll, the new bands, the weird stuff, it barely got a look in."),
("R", "And a few stubborn people looked at that and thought, well, the rules stop at the shoreline, don't they? So they put a transmitter on a ship, anchored it off the coast in international waters, and started broadcasting."),
("B", "That was Radio Caroline. A ship, a transmitter, a handful of DJs, and suddenly millions of listeners who'd been starved of music had a station playing exactly what they wanted to hear."),
("R", "And here's the part I love. They weren't hiding. They were on the air, loud and proud, and the audience loved them for it. Tuning in to a ship in the North Sea felt like being let in on a secret that half the country was in on."),
("B", "It worked so well that the government eventually passed a law in nineteen sixty-seven to shut the ships down. And right around the same time, the BBC launched Radio One. A whole new national pop station."),
("R", "Which tells you something. The pirates didn't just get silenced. They changed the thing that was trying to silence them. The big, official station had to go and copy what the little rebels were already doing."),
("B", "That happens a lot, if you look for it. Somebody on the edge tries something, the people in the middle laugh, and then a few years later the middle quietly borrows the idea."),
("R", "Now, that's the ocean version. But there's a street-level version too, and honestly it's the one that gets me."),
("B", "Tell them about Springfield."),
("R", "So, in the nineteen-eighties, a man named Mbanna Kantako, who was blind, lived in public housing in Springfield, Illinois. He started broadcasting a tiny, low-power station from his own apartment. Just for his neighbours. Music, news, stories about their own lives that no commercial station was ever going to tell."),
("B", "A few watts. Barely reached a few blocks. But for the people in those blocks, it was the first time their own community sounded like itself on the radio."),
("R", "And that's really the heart of it, isn't it? It's not about power. It's not about how far the signal goes. It's about whose voice is coming out of the speaker."),
("B", "Mm. And then you've got people like Stephen Dunifer, in Berkeley, California, in the early nineties. He built small transmitters and handed them out, and taught people how to put up their own stations. The idea was simple. If the airwaves belong to everyone, then everyone should get a turn."),
("R", "He took that fight all the way into court. And in the long run, it helped push the conversation toward something real. Eventually, the United States set up a licensed low-power F.M. service, so small community stations could exist out in the open."),
("B", "So, in a way, the little stations won. Not completely. Not neatly. But the idea that a neighbourhood should have its own radio, that stuck."),
("R", "[warmly] And this is where I want to talk about why it matters for the art side of it, because that's what I care about."),
("B", "Go on."),
("R", "Think about who gets played on the big stations. Somebody has decided what sells. Somebody has done the maths. And that's fine, that's a business. But it means a lot of beautiful, strange, brand new things never get heard, because nobody's sure they'll sell."),
("B", "Right. The song recorded in a bedroom. The poem that's too long. The band that doesn't fit a genre. The story that's only funny if you were there."),
("R", "Pirate radio doesn't have to ask whether it'll sell. It only has to ask whether it's alive."),
("B", "And that's a very different question. You can take a chance on someone. You can play the first-ever song a friend has written, and a hundred strangers hear it before anyone's told them whether it's good."),
("R", "And some of those songs turn out to be wonderful. Some turn out to be a bit of a mess, honestly. And that's all fine, because the mess is part of how you find the wonderful."),
("B", "[laughs] Several of our own recordings are in the mess category."),
("R", "[laughs] Several of them are the mess category. But we're not going to apologise for it."),
("B", "Okay, so there's another side to this, and it's the one I feel most, which is community."),
("R", "Go on."),
("B", "When you listen to a big commercial station, you're a customer. You're a number. But when you listen to a little radio station that's made by the people around you, you're a neighbour. You might even know the DJ. You might be the DJ next week."),
("R", "It's the difference between a restaurant and a kitchen table."),
("B", "Yes! That's exactly it. At a kitchen table, you can wander in, you can pick up a spoon, you can say something dumb, and nobody throws you out."),
("R", "And that's what a place like Apo-jee-uh is, isn't it? A whole temporary town where people make things for each other. Camps, art, music, food, strange little rituals, all of it gifted. Nobody's selling you anything. They just built it because they thought you might like it."),
("B", "And radio fits that perfectly. It's the most generous medium there is. Anyone with a radio can listen, for free, no account, no password, no subscription. It just arrives."),
("R", "Out in the dark, with a dead phone and a long walk back to camp, a voice on the radio can feel like a hand on your shoulder."),
("B", "[softly] It really can."),
("R", "People have been using radio like that for a hundred years. News when nobody else would say it. Music when nobody else would play it. Company at three in the morning when the world feels very large and you feel very small."),
("B", "And a lot of the time, the people on the other end of the microphone are just as lonely as the ones listening. That's the secret. The DJ needs you too."),
("R", "Absolutely. A radio station without listeners is just a guy talking to himself in a room."),
("B", "Which, to be fair, is also a pretty good description of this station on a quiet night."),
("R", "[laughs] It is. It really is. But the point is, now there's you."),
("B", "So, here's what we'd love you to take away from all this."),
("R", "Go for it."),
("B", "One. Make something. It doesn't have to be good. It doesn't have to be finished. A song, a story, a mix, a strange little sound you recorded by accident. Make it, and let someone hear it."),
("R", "Two. Listen to your neighbours. Not just the loud ones. The quiet ones usually have the best stories."),
("B", "And three. If you ever find the door closed, and you really believe what you've got is worth hearing, build your own door. That's all pirate radio ever was. Somebody building a door."),
("R", "[warmly] That's beautiful. I'm going to try and not ruin it with a joke."),
("B", "You're going to ruin it with a joke."),
("R", "I'm going to ruin it with a tiny joke. Alright. Why did the pirate DJ get such a good reception?"),
("B", "Oh no."),
("R", "Because he was always on the right wavelength. Arr."),
("B", "[laughs] That was terrible. I'm so proud of you."),
("R", "Thank you. I'll be here all week. Technically all year."),
("B", "Alright, you've been listening to a little talk about pirate radio, and the way a few watts and a lot of nerve can bring a community together."),
("R", "This is K.R.A.P., ninety-seven point seven F.M., the official radio of Apo-jee-uh. I'm Rocky."),
("B", "And I'm Britta. Be loud. Be kind. Make something."),
("R", "And whatever you do, don't touch that dial."),
]

